menu "natKit node"

choice NATKIT_NODE_ROLE
    prompt "Node role"
    default NATKIT_ROLE_LEAF
    help
        Which of the three roles in the primary/secondary architecture this
        image is (EPIC TEC-NATKIT-20). One tree, three images.

        The role is a build-time choice rather than a runtime one on purpose:
        the whole point of the split is that a leaf never brings up WiFi, MQTT
        or NTP, so a leaf image should not even contain them. Switching role is
        a rebuild and a reflash.

config NATKIT_ROLE_LEAF
    bool "Leaf (secondary): sensor + ESP-NOW only"
    help
        A sensor node. Reads the IMU and sends frames to the primary over
        ESP-NOW. No WiFi association, no MQTT, no NTP -- it takes its time from
        the primary's timing broadcast.

config NATKIT_ROLE_PRIMARY
    bool "Primary: ESP-NOW hub, timing master, serial uplink"
    help
        The ESP-NOW hub. Holds the registry of which leaves it will listen to,
        reassembles their frames, is the timing master for the 1-second
        broadcast, and forwards everything over serial to the gateway.

config NATKIT_ROLE_GATEWAY
    bool "Gateway: serial in, WiFi/Ethernet + MQTT out"
    help
        The only internet-facing device. Reads the primary's serial stream and
        publishes it on the existing natKit MQTT topic contract
        (natKit/receiving/<Topic>-<id>-Json-<Schema>), so nothing server-side
        changes. Also the NTP client.

endchoice

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE
    bool "Build the ESP-NOW bench probe instead of the node role"
    default n
    help
        Replaces the node role with the TEC-NATKIT-23 measurement tool: reports
        this chip's ESP-NOW version and real payload limit, sweeps payload sizes,
        and measures send rate with the actual 524-byte IMU frame.

        It is a separate switch rather than a fourth role because it is not part
        of the architecture -- it is the instrument used to decide part of it.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE_RECEIVER
    bool "Probe acts as receiver (counts packets and sequence gaps)"
    depends on NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE
    default n
    help
        Flash one board as sender (default) and a second with this set to measure
        actual loss. Both must be on the same channel.

config NATKIT_CHANNEL_SURVEY
    bool "Primary surveys the band at boot and picks the quietest channel"
    default n
    help
        ⚠️ DEFAULT OFF, BECAUSE IT MEASURES THE WRONG THING AND CAN KILL THE
        LINK. It scores OTHER NETWORKS' 802.11 traffic, which is not what limits
        this rig: the interference that matters here is non-802.11 (the Thread
        Border Router board's own clocks) and is INVISIBLE to a promiscuous
        receiver. Observed: it chose a channel that was genuinely quiet by its own
        table, on which the leaf then could not be heard at all -- the leaf
        receiving beacons at -29 dBm with its clock locked, while the hub reported
        "no nodes yet".

        Measuring the ACTUAL LINK is what works. Sweeping channels and recording
        the hub's RSSI of a real leaf found -22 dBm and the full rate on 3 and 10,
        against -61 to -83 dBm and nothing on 9, 11 and 13. That is the survey
        this should become: have a leaf transmit on each channel and score what
        the hub hears, rather than scoring strangers.

        Left in the tree, off by default, because the mechanism is sound and the
        table it prints is genuinely useful next to a site survey -- it correctly
        found the two access-point clusters Zach's UniFi scan shows. It is the
        SCORING that is wrong, not the sweep.

        The original reasoning, still true: the primary cannot publish for the
        first ~33 s after a reset anyway, because NTP has not synced and every
        data frame is refused rather than published with a 1970 timestamp. A
        thirteen-channel survey fits inside that window.

        Worth doing because channel choice has been the single largest factor in
        this rig's throughput, and it is a property of the SITE. On one bench the
        hub's view of a leaf a few centimetres away ranged from -22 dBm and the
        full rate on channel 3 to -83 dBm and nothing at all on channel 11. A
        hard-coded number that is right in one room is wrong in the next, and the
        failure is silent -- it presents as a bad radio, not a bad channel.

        Leaves need no configuration for this: they already hop channels until
        they find a hub, so the primary is free to move.

        ⚠️ It finds CROWDED channels, not NOISY ones. Non-802.11 interference is
        invisible to a promiscuous receiver, and that is exactly what ruins
        channel 1 on the ESP Thread Border Router board -- its own clocks. Turn
        this off and pin NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL if a site needs a channel the
        survey will not choose.

config NATKIT_CHANNEL_SURVEY_DWELL_MS
    int "How long to listen on each channel (milliseconds)"
    default 2000
    range 200 5000
    help
        13 channels at 2000 ms is 26 s, which fits the ~33 s NTP window with room
        to spare. Shorter dwells make the survey cheaper and noisier: a channel
        whose access point beacons every 100 ms needs a few hundred milliseconds
        before it is distinguishable from a quiet one.

config NATKIT_TX_POWER_SWEEP
    bool "Leaf sweeps its transmit power at startup and keeps the lowest that works"
    default y
    help
        Unlike NATKIT_CHANNEL_SURVEY, this measures the RIGHT thing. That one
        scores strangers' 802.11 traffic and had to be defaulted off after it
        chose a channel our own link could not cross. This scores OUR link: an
        ESP-NOW unicast reports success only when the far side's MAC acknowledged
        it, so the success ratio at a given power is exactly the quantity that
        matters, measured on the real path with the real packets.

        ⚠️ LOWEST that works, not highest, and that is the point. Measured on this
        bench at a few centimetres: 19.5 dBm delivered 0.4 frames/s to the hub and
        2 dBm delivered 8.5, because ~+9 dBm arriving at a 2.4 GHz front end
        saturates it. Sweeping upward and taking the first level that works lands
        below the overload region automatically, whatever the spacing turns out to
        be -- which is better than a hard-coded power that is right on a bench and
        wrong in a room.

        Runs in the window where the primary cannot publish anyway (NTP unsynced),
        and uses the data frames already being sent as its probes, so it adds no
        traffic.

config NATKIT_TX_POWER_SWEEP_DWELL_MS
    int "How long to hold each power level (milliseconds)"
    default 1500
    range 300 10000
    help
        Seven levels at 1500 ms is about 10 s. At ~13 packets/s that is ~20
        acknowledgements per level, enough to tell 100% from 30% but not enough
        to resolve 95% from 90% -- which is the right precision, since the
        transition out of saturation is tens of dB wide.

# ⚠️ NO ACCEPTANCE THRESHOLD, AND NO MARGIN. Both were tried and both were wrong,
# so they are recorded here rather than left as an option someone re-adds.
#
# The threshold (NATKIT_TX_POWER_SWEEP_MIN_PCT, 90%) was unreachable: the measured
# table on this bench is 2.0 dBm at 13% acked and every higher level at 0%. It was
# therefore dead on every boot, falling through to the fallback -- undetected only
# because the fallback was the floor, which is also the correct answer here.
#
# The margin (NATKIT_TX_POWER_SWEEP_MARGIN_STEPS, 2) was an attempt to fix the
# near-far problem, where the leaf closer to the hub captures the receiver and the
# far one is squeezed out. Raising both leaves uniformly made it WORSE, not better:
# 10.6 frames/s each fell to 2.6 and 6.2. Near-far is about the DIFFERENCE in
# arriving power, so a margin applied to everyone cancels out and only adds
# saturation. Equalising received power needs the hub to say what it is hearing,
# which is a feedback path this architecture does not yet have.

config NATKIT_TX_POWER_QUARTER_DBM
    int "Radio transmit power (quarter-dBm, 0 = leave at the default)"
    default 0
    range 0 80
    help
        ⚠️ ON A BENCH, FULL POWER CAN BE WORSE THAN LOW POWER, which is the
        opposite of the intuition and cost a long time to consider.

        At the IDF default of 78-80 quarter-dBm (19.5-20 dBm) and a few
        centimetres of separation, the signal arriving at the other board is
        around +9 dBm once free-space loss is subtracted. That is far above any
        2.4 GHz receiver's linear range -- the front end compresses, the AGC
        mis-reports, and packets are lost while the RSSI reads like a distant
        transmitter. Symptoms seen on this rig that fit: a hub reporting -83 dBm
        for a leaf 3 cm away whose own view of the hub was a healthy -24, RSSI
        for the same pair jumping between -16 and -83 with nothing moved, and
        reciprocity apparently violated (a passive path cannot do that, so the
        fault has to be in a receiver).

        Set this LOW for bench work -- 8 quarter-dBm is 2 dBm -- and leave it at 0
        for a deployment, where the nodes are metres apart and every dB helps.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL
    int "ESP-NOW channel"
    default 3
    range 1 13
    help
        Both ends must match. A channel mismatch presents as every packet sending
        successfully while nothing is received, which reads as total loss rather
        than as a misconfiguration.

        ⚠️ 3, CHOSEN BY SWEEPING AND MEASURING, not by reading a chart. A UniFi
        site survey showed two strong access-point clusters centred on 6 and 11
        with only a weak wide signal across 1-5, and the hub's view of a leaf
        agreed:

          channel   hub hears leaf   delivered
          3         -22 dBm          10 frames/s  <- the full rate
          6         -21 .. -57 dBm   varies
          9         -61 dBm          0
          11        -83 dBm          0
          13        nothing          0
          1         -82 dBm          jammed by the Thread BR board itself

        So 1 is unusable for a board reason, 6 and 11 for a site reason, and 3 is
        what is left. NONE of that is a property of the firmware: re-survey and
        re-sweep on a new site rather than trusting this number, and compare the
        two directions' RSSI before blaming the radio -- that comparison found
        three separate RF faults in one session.

        ⚠️ THE ORIGINAL FINDING, 1 vs 11, WAS 66 dB. On the ESP Thread Border
        Router board, channel 1 is unusable: the S3 heard leaves a few
        centimetres away at -82 dBm and received 4-8 frames against sequence
        numbers in the hundreds. Moving to channel 11 -- changing nothing else --
        took the same link to -16 dBm, 285 frames at the full 5/s, and 12 gaps.
        Channel 1 spans 2401-2423 MHz, which is where a 25 MHz crystal's 96th and
        97th harmonics land, so a board full of clocks is a plausible source.

        This cost most of a session to find because the symptom looks like a
        hardware fault: transmit worked (the leaves heard the hub at -25 dBm)
        while receive did not, and an antenna cannot do that -- a passive path is
        reciprocal. Both transmit powers were measured equal (19.5 and 20.0 dBm),
        which is what made "the receiver is being jammed" the only explanation
        left. If a future board behaves this way, MOVE CHANNEL BEFORE SUSPECTING
        THE RADIO.

        Deliberately NOT "depends on NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE", even though only the
        probe reads it. Every source file in main/ is compiled into every image
        (the invariant that stops a role from silently rotting), so a symbol that
        disappears with its switch is a build break for the other three roles --
        which is exactly what it was: espnow_probe.cpp used this as a VALUE, so
        leaf, primary and gateway all failed to compile with "was not declared in
        this scope". The receiver switch above survives its dependency only
        because it is read with #if, where an absent symbol is a legal 0.

# Per-report intervals, in microseconds. 0 DISABLES that report entirely.
#
# These exist because the hub does not deliver what it is asked for, and does not
# do so differently per report -- so one shared number cannot land four reports on
# one rate. See enableReports() in bno08x.cpp for the measured tables.
#
# Rule for reading the numbers: the frame builder takes a 100 Hz snapshot of each
# sensor's latest value, so ABOVE 100 Hz is merely wasted and BELOW 100 Hz leaves
# stale values in samples. 100 is a floor, not a target.

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_ACCEL_US
    int "Accelerometer report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_GYRO_US
    int "Gyroscope report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_MAG_US
    int "Magnetometer report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 9000
    range 0 1000000
    help
        ⚠️ 9000, NOT 10000, AND IT IS NOT ASKING FOR A HIGHER RATE. The hub
        delivers ~100 Hz either way. What changes is the PHASE: a report arriving
        at exactly the sample rate beats against the sampler, so some 10 ms slots
        get two of them and some get none. Measured at a 10 ms request, the
        magnetometer ran at 100.0 Hz and yet only 82% of samples carried a fresh
        magnetic field. At 9 ms it still runs at 99.9 Hz and 100% of samples are
        fresh.

        Only the magnetometer needs this. Accelerometer over-delivers at ~128 Hz
        and so cannot alias; gyroscope and rotation happen to land clear of it.
        Asking all three for 9 ms was tried and is much worse -- gyro and rotation
        snap to ~175 Hz, the total passes the transport ceiling, and accel and mag
        are starved back to 89 and 81 Hz.

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_QUAT_US
    int "Rotation vector report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_ACCEL_US
    int "Accelerometer report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_GYRO_US
    int "Gyroscope report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_MAG_US
    int "Magnetometer report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 9000
    range 0 1000000
    help
        ⚠️ 9000, NOT 10000, AND IT IS NOT ASKING FOR A HIGHER RATE. The hub
        delivers ~100 Hz either way. What changes is the PHASE: a report arriving
        at exactly the sample rate beats against the sampler, so some 10 ms slots
        get two of them and some get none. Measured at a 10 ms request, the
        magnetometer ran at 100.0 Hz and yet only 82% of samples carried a fresh
        magnetic field. At 9 ms it still runs at 99.9 Hz and 100% of samples are
        fresh.

        Only the magnetometer needs this. Accelerometer over-delivers at ~128 Hz
        and so cannot alias; gyroscope and rotation happen to land clear of it.
        Asking all three for 9 ms was tried and is much worse -- gyro and rotation
        snap to ~175 Hz, the total passes the transport ceiling, and accel and mag
        are starved back to 89 and 81 Hz.

config NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_QUAT_US
    int "Rotation vector report interval (us, 0 = disabled)"
    default 10000
    range 0 1000000

config NATKIT_IMU_ENABLE_MAGNETOMETER
    bool "Enable the magnetometer report"
    default y
    help
        The magnetometer is enabled but is NOT on the wire -- the bulk schema has
        no field for it. This exists to measure what it costs, because the four
        reports share a budget rather than being independent.

        Measured at a 100 Hz request, all four enabled: accel 116, gyro 93,
        mag 90, quat 93 Hz, ~392 reports/s in total. Asking for 200 Hz instead
        did not scale any of them -- gyro and quat rose to ~174 while accel FELL
        from 116 to 63 and mag from 90 to 62. A request for more of one report
        starves the others, which is the signature of a shared ceiling.

        Not the SPI clock: 3 MHz produced numbers identical to 1 MHz.

config NATKIT_IMU_REPORT_INTERVAL_US
    int "Nominal report interval (microseconds) -- REFERENCE ONLY"
    default 10000
    range 2500 1000000
    help
        The rate the system is nominally built around. ⚠️ It does NOT configure
        anything any more -- the four NATKIT_IMU_INTERVAL_*_US options do, because
        one shared number cannot land four reports on one rate. Kept because other
        code compares against "the intended rate".

        ⚠️ 10000 us (100 Hz) since #380. It was 18000 to match ../embeded, and
        that "compatibility" was preserving a bug: the frame header has always
        declared 100 Hz while the sample loop ran at 50. Sampling at 100 Hz
        without asking the hub for reports at 100 Hz would simply duplicate
        values -- the same lie in a new place -- so these two move together.

        ⚠️ The hub does NOT honour this equally across reports. Measured on
        TEC-NATKIT-22 against an 18000 us request: accel ~64 Hz, gyro/mag/
        rotation ~98 Hz. Accel is the one at risk of not reaching 100 Hz, so
        verify the per-report rates on the leaf console rather than trusting the
        request. The BNO08x will not honour arbitrarily small values.

config NATKIT_IMU_SAMPLE_INTERVAL_US
    int "Frame sample interval (microseconds)"
    default 10000
    range 1000 1000000
    help
        How often the leaf snapshots the sensor's latest readings into a frame
        sample. 10000 us = 100 Hz.

        ⚠️ 10000 since #380. It was 20000 (50 Hz) to match DELAY_BETWEEN_SAMPLES
        in ../embeded/include/BoardConfig.hpp -- but that firmware ALSO declares
        IMU_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ 100 in every frame header, so both firmwares have been
        claiming twice the rate they sample. Matching the declaration is the fix;
        matching the old interval was preserving the bug.

        This is NOT the sensor's report rate (that is
        NATKIT_IMU_REPORT_INTERVAL_US, configured on the hub). The four SH2 reports
        arrive asynchronously at their own rates, so a frame sample is a snapshot
        merged across them -- which is what the schema carries and what the current
        firmware sends. Treat it as a compatibility setting.

config NATKIT_IMU_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME
    int "Samples per frame"
    default 10
    range 1 16
    help
        How many samples are batched into one canonical frame. 10 matches
        IMU_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME in ../embeded/include/kafkaTopic.hpp, which makes the
        frame 24 + 50 * 10 = 524 bytes -- the size TEC-NATKIT-23 measured against
        the 1470-byte ESP-NOW ceiling when deciding that one frame is one packet.

        Raising this changes that arithmetic: at 16 samples a frame is 824 bytes,
        still one packet, but the transmit queue item grows with it. Above 28 the
        frame would no longer fit in a single ESP-NOW packet and the decision this
        whole slice rests on would need revisiting.

config NATKIT_IMU_DECLARED_RATE_HZ
    int "Declared sample rate in the frame header (Hz)"
    default 100
    range 1 1000
    help
        The sampleRateHz advertised in each frame header. 100 matches
        IMU_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ in ../embeded/include/kafkaTopic.hpp.

        Informational by design, and deliberately NOT derived from the sample
        interval above: every sample carries its own timestamp, and that is the
        source of truth for a viewer's time axis.

        ⚠️ It is now TRUE, which it was not before #380. Both firmwares declared
        100 here while sampling at 50, so anything trusting this field -- a
        resampler, a feature window, an exported Parquet's metadata -- was working
        from double the real rate. Consumers using per-sample timestamps were
        always fine. If this is ever changed again, change the sample interval
        with it.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_TX_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "Leaf transmit queue depth (frames)"
    default 8
    range 2 64
    help
        How many packets the leaf will hold while the radio is busy or the primary
        is away. When it fills, the OLDEST packet is dropped: for a sensor stream
        the freshest frame is the valuable one, and the gap is detectable on the far
        side from seqNo.

        The queue is what keeps the sample loop from ever stalling on the radio,
        which is a requirement of TEC-NATKIT-24 rather than a tuning choice -- so
        the interesting number is not how deep it is but that a full queue drops
        instead of blocking. Each slot costs a full packet buffer, so depth trades
        RAM for how long a primary can be away before frames are lost.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_HEARTBEAT_MS
    int "Leaf heartbeat interval (milliseconds, 0 = off)"
    default 2000
    range 0 60000
    help
        How often the leaf sends its status on the same path as the data: uptime,
        heap, frames built/sent/dropped, sensor report count, hub resets and the
        four calibration accuracies.

        It exists so a silent node is distinguishable from a node with nothing to
        say. It also makes a drop ATTRIBUTABLE: frames the leaf dropped from a full
        queue are its own back-pressure, not the primary's loss, and from the
        receiving side alone the two are indistinguishable.

config NATKIT_NODE_PRESENCE_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long a node may go unheard before the hub stops counting it present"
    default 30000
    range 1000 600000
    help
        The window behind `nodes_present` in the hub's status frame. A node the
        primary has not heard from -- no data frame, no heartbeat, no announce --
        for longer than this stops being counted as part of the fleet, and the hub
        logs the transition once rather than printing a growing "silent" figure
        forever among everything else it prints.

        It does NOT touch the registry. The roster is persistent on purpose, and a
        node dropping off the air is exactly the case a sealed rig must keep
        accepting when it comes back.

        30 s by default, and the default is set by the known failure rather than by
        the heartbeat: leaves on this rig go quiet for up to 18 s and return
        (TEC-NATKIT-46), so anything near the 2 s heartbeat would spend its life
        flapping and teach an operator to ignore it. A board that is actually gone
        stays gone for hours (TEC-NATKIT-81), so nothing is lost by waiting.

config NATKIT_TIME_BEACON_MS
    int "Primary timing broadcast interval (milliseconds)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        How often the primary broadcasts its timing beacon (#340 /
        TEC-NATKIT-17). One second is the #319 whiteboard's figure, and it is
        also what the old hub-discovery beacon ran at -- which this REPLACES
        rather than joins, so there is one 1 Hz broadcast from the primary
        instead of two that could drift apart.

        Each beacon is followed immediately by a follow-up packet carrying the
        beacon's actual transmit time, so this interval costs two small packets
        per second, not one. Shortening it fills a leaf's 32-sample fit window
        faster and costs airtime; lengthening it makes the window span more real
        temperature drift, which the linear fit is less able to follow.

config NATKIT_TIME_SYNC_PROBE_MS
    int "Leaf sync report and probe interval (milliseconds)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        How often a leaf tells the primary what its clock fit is, and then probes
        so the primary can check it.

        The probe is what makes the accuracy figure independent: without it the
        only evidence about the fit comes from the fit's own residual, which is
        self-consistency rather than correctness. The primary compares the
        probe's measured arrival against what the leaf's reported fit predicted,
        and the difference is the sync error.

config NATKIT_HOLD_RCP_IN_RESET
    bool "Hold the ESP32-H2 radio co-processor in reset (Thread BR board)"
    default n
    help
        The ESP Thread Border Router board carries an ESP32-H2 a few
        millimetres from the S3, and stock RCP firmware transmits 802.15.4 in
        the SAME 2.4 GHz band ESP-NOW uses. That makes it a candidate in-band
        interferer sitting inside the S3's own antenna near-field.

        Measured on this board: the S3 is HEARD fine by the leaves (beacons
        arrive, their clocks lock) while their unicasts back are almost never
        acknowledged -- a working transmitter and a deaf receiver, which is what
        a nearby interferer looks like. Disabling the Ethernet uplink changed
        nothing, so the W5500 is not the cause.

        This is an experiment with a measurement attached. If holding the H2
        down restores reception, this board needs the co-processor managed
        rather than left running whatever was flashed to it last.

config NATKIT_RCP_RESET_GPIO
    int "GPIO wired to the co-processor's reset"
    default 7
    help
        7 on the ESP Thread Border Router board (CONFIG_PIN_TO_RCP_RESET in
        Espressif's own basic_thread_border_router), taken from
        ~/code/esp-thread-br rather than guessed.

config NATKIT_PRIMARY_ETH_UPLINK
    bool "Primary uplinks over WIRED ETHERNET and publishes to MQTT itself"
    default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default n
    help
        The primary keeps being the ESP-NOW hub and timing master, and reaches
        the broker over a wire. No serial link, no second board, and -- unlike
        NATKIT_PRIMARY_WIFI_UPLINK below -- no radio contention at all.

        This is the answer to the question the epic assumed away. #373 measured
        one chip doing ESP-NOW plus associated WiFi losing ~85% of its ESP-NOW
        frames: acknowledged at the MAC layer, then dropped above it by a WiFi
        task busy servicing the association. Ethernet is OFF THE RADIO, so
        ESP-NOW keeps a channel we choose and there is no association to inherit
        a channel from.

        ⚠️ DEFAULTS ON FOR THE ESP32-S3, because the only S3 in this project is
        the board with the Ethernet daughterboard, and losing this setting is
        silent: the primary comes up with no uplink at all, the console shows no
        Ethernet or MQTT lines, and the uplink counters happily report frames
        "sent" down a UART nobody is reading. That cost a debugging cycle twice,
        both times after deleting a generated sdkconfig to pick up a changed
        default.

        ⚠️ ESP32-S3 ONLY, and not because of a software limit: the S3 has no
        internal Ethernet MAC, so this is a W5500 (MAC+PHY behind SPI) rather
        than a PHY on an EMAC. The classic ESP32 would use its EMAC and a
        different driver entirely.

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_HOST
    int "W5500 SPI host"
    default 2
    range 0 2
    help
        2 (SPI3_HOST) on the ESP Thread Border Router board.

        Deliberately NOT the sensor's host: the BNO08x is on SPI2_HOST
        (board_config.hpp). This board carries no sensor, but sharing a bus
        between a 1 MHz hub that has already proven fussy about timing
        (TEC-NATKIT-22) and a 36 MHz Ethernet controller is a class of problem
        worth never having.

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_SCLK_GPIO
    int "W5500 SCLK GPIO"
    default 21

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_MOSI_GPIO
    int "W5500 MOSI GPIO"
    default 45

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_MISO_GPIO
    int "W5500 MISO GPIO"
    default 38

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_CS_GPIO
    int "W5500 CS GPIO"
    default 41

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_INT_GPIO
    int "W5500 interrupt GPIO"
    default 39

config NATKIT_ETH_PHY_RST_GPIO
    int "W5500 reset GPIO"
    default 40

config NATKIT_ETH_SPI_CLOCK_MHZ
    int "W5500 SPI clock (MHz)"
    default 36
    range 5 80
    help
        Every default above is KNOWN-GOOD rather than derived: they are the
        values Espressif's own basic_thread_border_router ships for this board,
        and Zach confirmed that firmware working on this exact hardware. Taken
        from ~/code/esp-thread-br rather than read off a schematic, which is why
        they can be trusted without a continuity check.

        ⚠️ Pins on this board that are already spoken for, so nothing else
        wanders onto them:
          19, 20        native USB Serial/JTAG (the S3 console)
          26-32         SPI flash and PSRAM
          21, 38-41, 45 this W5500
          7, 8          ESP32-H2 radio co-processor reset and boot
          17, 18        ESP32-H2 radio co-processor UART

config NATKIT_PRIMARY_WIFI_UPLINK
    bool "Primary associates with WiFi and publishes to MQTT itself (#373)"
    default n
    help
        A deliberately worse architecture, built to find out whether the better
        one is necessary (TEC-NATKIT-30).

        The primary keeps being the ESP-NOW hub and the timing master, and ALSO
        associates with an access point and publishes straight to the broker.
        No serial link, no second board, no gateway. If this works well enough,
        the serial mux (TEC-NATKIT-25) and the gateway (TEC-NATKIT-26) are
        complexity the rig may not need, and TEC-NATKIT-27's adopt-or-discard
        decision changes shape.

        ⚠️ THE CHANNEL IS THE CATCH. ESP-NOW requires every peer on one channel,
        and an associated station does not choose its channel -- the AP does. So
        with this set the primary stops pinning NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL, adds its
        peers on channel 0 ("follow the interface"), and leaves hop channels
        until they find it. A leaf pinned to the wrong channel is a silent
        failure: every send succeeds locally and nothing is ever received.

        ⚠️ Power save must stay off, which gatewayWifiStart does. An associated
        station defaults to sleeping between the AP's beacons, and ESP-NOW
        packets arriving while it sleeps are simply missed -- so a rig that looks
        like it has a bad radio may only have a dozing one.

config NATKIT_REGISTRY_SEALED
    bool "Seal the primary's node registry at boot"
    default n
    help
        A sealed registry accepts only the nodes already on its roster and
        counts and drops everything else. An open one learns any node that
        turns up and remembers it in NVS.

        Open by default is deliberate: a registry that has to be populated by
        hand before anything works is a registry people disable. Quietly
        accepting a stranger is the worse failure only AFTER you have told the
        rig who its nodes are -- so seal it once a rig is the set of nodes you
        want, and a neighbouring rig on the same channel stops being able to
        appear in your recordings.

        Applied at every boot, so this build option is the state of the rig
        rather than a one-way switch. It is how a fielded primary is frozen
        while there is no downward command channel; that path is
        TEC-NATKIT-26's decision.

config NATKIT_REGISTRY_CLEAR_ON_BOOT
    bool "Forget every remembered node at boot"
    default n
    help
        Bench control. Wipes the persisted roster on startup so a rig can be
        re-learned from scratch without erasing the whole NVS partition.

        Leave this off in anything real: combined with a sealed registry it
        would produce a primary that boots having forgotten every node and
        refuses to learn any, which rejects the entire rig.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_UART_NUM
    int "Primary uplink UART (0 shares the console; 1 is the real link)"
    default 1
    range 0 1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    range 0 2
    help
        ⚠️ THE ESP32-C3 HAS ONLY TWO UARTs (0 and 1), not three, hence the
        narrower range. The default of 1 is valid everywhere, so this guards the
        menuconfig path rather than the default one -- but a 2 selected by hand
        on a C3 fails the same way an out-of-range GPIO does: an abort inside
        driver setup, before there is any console output to explain it.


        Which UART carries the primary's framed stream to the gateway
        (TEC-NATKIT-25).

        1 is the design: a dedicated wire, with UART0 left as the human console.
        This matters more than it looks -- #340 measured the primary's console
        blocking its own task for ~87 ms every second on status lines alone, and
        the uplink cannot share that budget.

        0 is a BRING-UP mode for a bench with no USB-to-TTL adapter on it: the
        binary frames are interleaved into the USB console alongside the log
        text, and the reader resynchronises past the text. That works because
        resync-after-garbage is a requirement of the protocol anyway, so this
        mode exercises it hard. The logger is routed through the same lock as
        the frame writer in this mode, or a log line landing mid-frame would
        corrupt most of them.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_BAUD
    int "Primary uplink baud rate"
    default 921600
    range 9600 4000000
    help
        Budget this rather than picking it. Each node costs about
        (18 + 24 + 50 * samples-per-frame + 4) bytes per frame at
        1e6 / (sample interval * samples per frame) frames per second -- roughly
        2.7 KB/s at the defaults. A UART carries baud/10 bytes per second at 8N1.

        So 115200 (~11.5 KB/s) is about 4 nodes and 921600 (~92 KB/s) is about
        33. natVR stalled its pipeline running ~19 KB/s of frames into a 115200
        console, which is the whole reason this help text does arithmetic. The
        primary prints the same sum at boot with the configured values.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_TX_GPIO
    int "Primary uplink UART TX GPIO"
    default 9 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default 5 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default 26
    range 0 21 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    range 0 48
    help
        Ignored when the uplink shares UART0.

        ⚠️ PER-TARGET, AND EVERY TARGET THAT IS MISSING FROM THE LIST FAILS THE
        SAME WAY: it takes the fallback below, `uart_set_pin` rejects a GPIO the
        chip does not have, and the board REBOOTS IN A LOOP before anything else
        runs. There is no log to read, because nothing gets far enough to log.

        26/25 -- classic ESP32. Free on the ESP32-PICO-V3-02: no strapping
        function, not flash or PSRAM, and clear of every BNO08x pin in
        board_config.hpp.

        5/6 -- ESP32-C3 (TEC-NATKIT-87). The C3 has only GPIO 0-21, so it would
        inherit 26/25 and abort. Its budget: 11-17 are SPI flash (11 is
        VDD_SPI), 18/19 the native USB Serial/JTAG, 20/21 the UART0 console that
        sdkconfig.defaults.esp32c3 deliberately keeps there, 2/8/9 strapping
        pins sampled at reset (a UART line idles high), and 4 the status LED.
        That leaves 3, 5, 6, 7 and 10; 5/6 is the adjacent pair and 10/3 are the
        alternates. 5/6 are also MTDI/MTCK, so this forfeits EXTERNAL JTAG --
        which costs nothing, because the C3 debugs over its own USB
        Serial/JTAG on 18/19.

        9/10 -- ESP32-S3. It has no GPIO 22-25 at all, and 26-32 there are the
        SPI flash and PSRAM pins, so the classic pair's "working" half would
        have been worse than the failing half. 9/10 is what is left after
        removing EVERYTHING already spoken for on the Thread Border Router
        board: 4 (status LED), 5/14/15/19/21/32 (the BNO08x pins in
        board_config.hpp), 19/20 (native USB Serial/JTAG), 26-32 (SPI flash and
        PSRAM), 33-37 (octal PSRAM where fitted), 21/38-41/45 (the W5500), 7/8
        (H2 co-processor reset and boot), 17/18 (H2 co-processor UART), 39-42
        (JTAG), 43/44 (UART0), and 0/3/45/46 (strapping).

        ⚠️ 5 was the obvious adjacent choice and is WRONG: `kBnoSck` is
        GPIO_NUM_5 in board_config.hpp for this target. Nothing would have
        broken today -- a primary has no sensor and a leaf has no uplink, so the
        two are never configured in one image -- but choosing a pin another
        header already claims is how the GPIO 4 problem below got made in the
        first place, and "it does not collide yet" is exactly the sentence that
        preceded it.

        ⚠️ UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE, and honestly so: the S3 runs the ETHERNET
        uplink, so it never calls uart_set_pin at all (see the guard in
        uplinkStart). Nothing on this bench exercises an S3 serial uplink, and
        these two numbers are reasoned from the pin list above rather than
        measured against a schematic. Check them before wiring an S3 primary to
        a gateway.

        ⚠️ THE S3 USED TO DEFAULT TO 4, WHICH IS THE STATUS LED'S PIN
        (`kStatusLedGpio`, board_config.hpp). statusLedRestore() runs in
        app_main BEFORE the role dispatch, so RMT claims GPIO 4 on every role
        and every target. It never collided in practice only because
        uplinkStart() skips uart_set_pin entirely when the uplink publishes over
        the network, and the S3 defaults to the Ethernet uplink -- so the fault
        was armed to appear the first time somebody turned Ethernet OFF to use
        the serial uplink, which is exactly what the two-board WiFi rig does.
        Moved off it while nothing was wired to it.

        ⚠️ THE PROSE HERE HAS BEEN WRONG BEFORE, so trust the defaults above
        rather than the paragraphs. The previous version of this help
        recommended 17/18 for the S3 while the actual default was 4/5 -- and
        17/18 are the ESP32-H2 co-processor's UART on that board (see
        NATKIT_ETH_SPI_CLOCK_MHZ), so following the advice would have been worse
        than ignoring it. The `default` lines are the contract; everything else
        in this help is commentary on how they were chosen.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_RX_GPIO
    int "Primary uplink UART RX GPIO"
    default 10 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
    default 6 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default 25
    range 0 21 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    range 0 48
    help
        See the TX pin above for the per-target reasoning and the pin budgets.

        ⚠️ BOTH ENDS OF THE WIRE READ THESE TWO SYMBOLS, and that is what makes
        one setting describe a two-board link: each board drives its own TX and
        listens on its own RX, so the jumpers are a CROSSOVER between two
        different pin numbers when the boards are different targets. An ESP32
        hub feeding a C3 gateway is ESP32 GPIO 26 -> C3 GPIO 6 for data, and C3
        GPIO 5 -> ESP32 GPIO 25 for commands back. TX to TX is two push-pull
        outputs on one net, and the symptom -- garbage the CRC silently
        discards -- looks exactly like a baud mismatch.

        The receive direction is not read yet on the primary (the downward
        command path is TEC-NATKIT-26's remaining scope), but the pin is claimed
        now so the wiring and the enclosure (#320) do not have to change later.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "Primary uplink queue depth (frames)"
    default 16
    range 2 64
    help
        How many frames the primary holds while the wire is busy. When it fills,
        the OLDEST is dropped -- the same policy and the same reasoning as the
        leaf's radio queue, since for a sensor stream the freshest frame is the
        valuable one and both sequence numbers in the header make the gap
        detectable on the far side.

        Each slot costs a whole maximum-size frame, so depth trades RAM for how
        long the gateway can stall before frames are lost.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_WRITE_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long to wait for a frame to reach the wire (milliseconds)"
    default 100
    range 10 5000
    help
        Bounded on purpose. If the far end has stopped reading and the driver's
        buffer fills, the drain task has to come back and let the queue's drop
        policy run -- waiting forever would turn back-pressure into a silent
        stall, which is exactly the failure natVR hit.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_TX_BUFFER
    int "Uplink UART driver TX buffer (bytes)"
    default 2048
    range 256 16384

config NATKIT_UPLINK_RX_BUFFER
    int "Uplink UART driver RX buffer (bytes)"
    default 512
    range 256 16384

config NATKIT_UPLINK_STATUS_MS
    int "How often the primary sends node and primary status frames (ms)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        The uplink's own telemetry: per-node counters plus each node's clock fit,
        and the primary's health including what it dropped.

        The clock fit is not optional garnish -- the gateway cannot turn a data
        frame's device-relative timestamps into anything publishable without it,
        because the leaf deliberately does not rewrite its own timestamps (#340).

config NATKIT_IMU_LOG_INTERVAL_MS
    int "IMU sample log interval (milliseconds, 0 = off)"
    default 1000
    range 0 60000
    help
        How often the leaf prints the latest reading of each sensor, the report
        rate, the hub reset count and the heap. This is the whole output of the
        sensor slice until frames go over ESP-NOW (TEC-NATKIT-23 / -24), and it
        is what a soak watches.

config NATKIT_STATUS_LOG_INTERVAL_S
    int "Status log interval (seconds, 0 = off)"
    default 10
    range 0 3600
    help
        How often the idle status line (uptime, free heap, minimum free heap)
        is written to the console. It exists so a boot check or an overnight
        soak has something to watch, and so heap drift is visible without
        attaching a debugger -- the old firmware's std::bad_alloc came from the
        frame path allocating per copy, which a flat free-heap line would have
        made obvious.

endmenu
